INT. CHEESE AGING CAVE - NIGHT SHIFT

A limestone cave lit by strings of warm bulbs. Shelves of wheels stretch into the dark, each stamped with a date. The air is cold and smells of earth and milk.

ANOUK LEFEBVRE (30s, affineur, rubber apron, hairnet, a wooden mallet in her hand) taps wheel after wheel and listens to each one. DMITRI VOLKOV (30s, new delivery driver, still in his jacket) stands holding a clipboard, trying not to touch anything. PATRICE ADEYEMI (50s, owner, white lab coat, reading glasses on a chain) sits on an overturned crate at the end of the aisle, eating a rind.

DMITRI
Mademoiselle Lefebvre? I have the invoice.

ANOUK
Anouk. Put it on the crate.

DMITRI
Anouk. Where should I--

ANOUK
There.

He puts the clipboard down. She taps another wheel, and tilts her head.

ANOUK (CONT'D)
That one is not ready.

DMITRI
How can you tell?

ANOUK
It sounds like it's holding its breath.

Patrice chuckles into his rind.

PATRICE
She says that about people too.

ANOUK
Patrice.

PATRICE
I'm only telling the boy.

DMITRI
My name is Dmitri.

PATRICE
Dmitri. She says it about the ones she likes.

Anouk turns on him with the mallet raised. Patrice lifts his hands in mock surrender.

ANOUK
Go and count the Comté, old man.

PATRICE
At this hour?

ANOUK
At any hour.

Patrice goes, slowly, rind in hand. His steps fade. Anouk and Dmitri are left alone with the humming bulbs and the sleeping wheels.

DMITRI
He likes you.

ANOUK
He's my father's cousin.

DMITRI
That's not what I meant.

She says nothing. She taps a wheel. Dmitri watches her hands.

DMITRI (CONT'D)
Can I ask you something?

ANOUK
Ask.

DMITRI
Why do you work nights?

ANOUK
The cheese doesn't care. The cheese is the same at noon.

DMITRI
That's not the answer.

ANOUK
What is the answer, then?

DMITRI
I think you can't sleep.

Her mallet stops. She turns, finally. She looks at him, fully, for the first time since he came in.

ANOUK
And you? Why do you drive nights?

DMITRI
I told them I needed the money.

ANOUK
Do you?

DMITRI
No.

ANOUK
So why?

DMITRI
Because at this hour, everyone I deliver to is kind. They open the door in their bathrobe. They say thank you like they mean it.

ANOUK
And during the day?

DMITRI
During the day nobody looks at me.

She sets down the mallet. She wipes her hands on her apron and steps toward him, and the cave is so quiet that he can hear her breath.

ANOUK
Dmitri.

DMITRI
Yes?

ANOUK
I looked at you the moment you walked in.

DMITRI
You said put it on the crate.

ANOUK
I said it so you would not see my face.

He takes a step. A wheel of cheese on the shelf behind her shifts with a small, soft crack. She flinches, and then laughs, and the laugh goes wrong in her throat.

ANOUK (CONT'D)
No. No, I can't. I can't do this tonight.

DMITRI
Anouk--

ANOUK
You come in here in your jacket with your clipboard and you ask me why I can't sleep, as if it were a polite question!

DMITRI
It was a polite question.

ANOUK
It was not polite! Nothing about you is polite! You look at me like I'm a thing that might stay!

DMITRI
Isn't that allowed?

ANOUK
Not by me!

She grabs the mallet and strikes the nearest wheel, hard. It rings like a bell. She strikes another, and another, and the whole cave sings with her fury, shelves trembling.

Dmitri doesn't flinch. He takes the mallet from her hand, gently, and lays it on the crate beside the invoice.

DMITRI
You said it sounds like it's holding its breath.

ANOUK
(shaking)
Yes.

DMITRI
So breathe.

She does, once, like a diver surfacing. Her forehead drops against his jacket. His hand, uncertain, finds her hairnet, then her back.
